    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one actually observed. A small p-value means the observ...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "A p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one actually observed. A small p-value means the observed data would be unusual if the null hypothesis were true, which is evidence against it ? it is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, and it says nothing directly about the size or business importance of an effect. A statistically significant result (say p = 0.02) can still correspond to a trivially small effect, especially with a large sample, while a practically meaningful effect can fail to reach significance with a small sample. Interpreting a p-value properly always requires looking at it alongside the significance level, effect size, and confidence interval.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one obtained from the data. It measures how incompatible the observed data are with the null hypothesis under the assumptions of the statistical test.\n\nA small p-value provides evidence against the null hypothesis, while a large p-value means there is not enough evidence to reject it at the chosen significance level.\n\n**What a p-value does not tell you:**\n\n- It is **not** the probability that the null hypothesis is true.\n- It is **not** the probability that the alternative hypothesis is true.\n- It does not tell you the size or practical importance of an effect.\n- It does not tell you the probability that the observed result occurred by chance in the everyday sense.\n- A statistically significant result does not necessarily mean the effect is practically important.\n- A non-significant result does not prove that there is no effect.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a study compares two treatments and obtains `p = 0.03`. At a significance level of `0.05`, the result would typically be considered statistically significant, so we would reject the null hypothesis.\n\nThis does **not** mean there is a 3% probability that the null hypothesis is true. Instead, assuming the null hypothesis and model assumptions are correct, it means data this extreme or more extreme would occur about 3% of the time.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> A p-value measures how unusual the observed data would be under the null hypothesis. A small p-value provides evidence against the null, but it does not give the probability that the null is true, the probability that the result occurred by chance, or the practical size of the effect.",
