    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "No. Failing to reject H0 only means the sample didn't provide enough evidence against it at the chosen significance level ? it does not prove H0 true, and it does not mean the alte...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "No. Failing to reject H0 only means the sample didn't provide enough evidence against it at the chosen significance level ? it does not prove H0 true, and it does not mean the alternative is false. A non-significant result can come from a genuinely negligible effect, but just as easily from high variance, an underpowered sample, or noisy measurement. The correct phrasing is \"the data do not provide sufficient evidence against H0,\" not \"H0 is true\" or \"there is no effect.\"",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nNo. Failing to reject the null hypothesis does not prove that the null hypothesis is true, and it does not prove that the alternative hypothesis is false. It means the available data do not provide sufficiently strong evidence against the null hypothesis under the chosen test and significance level.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we test:\n\n```text\nH0: The new treatment has no effect.\nH1: The new treatment has an effect.\n```\n\nIf the test produces `p = 0.20` with `alpha = 0.05`, we fail to reject `H0`.\n\nThe correct conclusion is:\n\n> There is insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\n\nIt would be incorrect to say:\n\n> The null hypothesis has been proven true.\n\nThe study may simply have had insufficient statistical power, too much variability, too small a sample, or an effect that is genuinely very small.\n\n**Confidence and power:**\n\nIf ruling out a meaningful effect is important, it is useful to examine confidence intervals, equivalence tests, or non-inferiority tests rather than treating a non-significant p-value as proof of no effect.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Failing to reject the null means the data did not provide enough evidence against it at the chosen significance level. It does not prove the null is true or the alternative is false. To make stronger claims about the absence of a meaningful effect, I would examine confidence intervals and consider equivalence or non-inferiority testing.",
