    subcategorySlug: "statistics-and-probability",
    experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
    difficulty: "EASY",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis (a false positive) ? concluding there's an effect when there isn't one ? and its rate is controlled by the significance level ?....",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis (a false positive) ? concluding there's an effect when there isn't one ? and its rate is controlled by the significance level ?. A Type II error is failing to reject a false null hypothesis (a false negative) ? missing a real effect ? and its rate is denoted ?. Statistical power is 1 ? ?, the probability of correctly detecting a real effect of a given size. Power increases with larger sample size, larger true effect size, lower measurement noise, and a higher chosen ? (at the cost of more false positives), so there's an inherent trade-off between the two error types for a fixed sample size.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA Type I error occurs when we reject a true null hypothesis, while a Type II error occurs when we fail to reject a false null hypothesis. The significance level `alpha` controls the maximum Type I error rate under the test procedure, while statistical power is the probability of detecting a specified real effect and is equal to `1 - beta`, where `beta` is the Type II error probability for that effect and design.\n\n**The two errors:**\n\n```text\nType I error (false positive)\nH0 is true, but we reject H0.\n\nType II error (false negative)\nH0 is false, but we fail to reject H0.\n```\n\n**Statistical power:**\n\nPower is:\n\n```text\nPower = 1 - beta\n```\n\nFor example, if a study has 80% power to detect a particular effect size under its assumptions, then the Type II error probability for that effect and design is 20%.\n\n**What affects power:**\n\n- Larger sample size generally increases power.\n- Larger true effect sizes are easier to detect.\n- Lower measurement noise increases power.\n- A higher significance level increases power but also increases Type I error risk.\n- Better experimental design can improve power.\n- One-sided and two-sided tests can have different power characteristics.\n\n**Example:**\n\nIn a medical trial, rejecting an effective treatment's null hypothesis when it is actually ineffective is a Type I error. Failing to detect a genuinely effective treatment is a Type II error.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Type I error is a false positive: rejecting a true null hypothesis. Type II error is a false negative: failing to reject a false null hypothesis. The significance level controls Type I error, while statistical power is `1 - beta`, the probability of detecting a specified true effect under the study design.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the statistical concept defined accurately.", "They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.", "They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer."],
