    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "The Central Limit Theorem says that the sampling distribution of the mean of a sufficiently large number of independent, identically distributed random variables approaches a norma...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "The Central Limit Theorem says that the sampling distribution of the mean of a sufficiently large number of independent, identically distributed random variables approaches a normal distribution, regardless of the shape of the original population distribution, as long as the population has finite variance. This matters in practice because it justifies using normal-distribution-based inference (confidence intervals, z-tests) for sample means even when the underlying data itself is skewed or non-normal, provided the sample size is reasonably large ? commonly cited as roughly 30+, though the number needed depends on how far the underlying distribution is from normal.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe Central Limit Theorem (CLT) states that, under common regularity conditions, the sampling distribution of the sample mean becomes approximately normal as the sample size increases, even when the underlying population distribution is not normal. Its mean is the population mean, and its standard deviation is the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size.\n\n\n\nThe CLT matters because it allows us to make approximate probability statements and construct confidence intervals about population means using normal-based methods even when the original data are not normally distributed, provided the assumptions and sample size are appropriate.\n\n**Example:**\n\nImagine individual customer purchase amounts are strongly right-skewed. A single purchase is not approximately normal. However, if we repeatedly take sufficiently large random samples and calculate the mean of each sample, the distribution of those sample means tends to become approximately bell-shaped.\n\n```text\nIndividual observations:\nSkewed / non-normal\n\n        v repeated random sampling\n\nSample means:\nApproximately normal for sufficiently large n\n```\n\nThe standard error of the sample mean decreases as sample size increases, which is why larger samples generally produce more precise estimates.\n\n**Important limitations:**\n\nThe CLT does not say that the original data become normally distributed. It concerns the distribution of a statistic such as the sample mean. Very heavy-tailed populations, dependence between observations, or small samples can require additional care.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> The Central Limit Theorem says that the sampling distribution of the sample mean tends toward a normal distribution as sample size increases, under appropriate conditions, even when the population itself is not normal. This is important because it provides the foundation for many confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, and statistical approximations.",
