    subcategorySlug: "statistics-and-probability",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A frequentist confidence interval is constructed so that, over repeated sampling, a given percentage (say 95%) of such intervals would contain the true parameter value; it is a sta...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "A frequentist confidence interval is constructed so that, over repeated sampling, a given percentage (say 95%) of such intervals would contain the true parameter value; it is a statement about the long-run behavior of the procedure, not a probability statement about the specific interval you computed containing the true value. A Bayesian credible interval is a direct probability statement: given the data and a prior, there's a 95% probability the parameter lies within that interval. The distinction matters because confidence intervals are frequently (mis)interpreted the Bayesian way in practice, which is technically incorrect under the frequentist framework, even though the numeric intervals are often similar for large samples with weak priors.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA confidence interval is a frequentist concept describing the behavior of an interval-producing procedure across repeated samples, while a credible interval is a Bayesian concept describing a posterior probability range for a parameter given the observed data and the chosen prior/model.\n\n**Confidence interval:**\n\nFor example, a 95% confidence interval is constructed using a method that, under its assumptions, captures the true parameter in approximately 95% of repeated samples in the long run.\n\nIt is not technically correct to say that there is a 95% probability that the fixed parameter is inside this particular frequentist interval.\n\n**Credible interval:**\n\nA 95% Bayesian credible interval can be interpreted as containing 95% of the posterior probability for the parameter, conditional on the data and the Bayesian model and prior.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we estimate a conversion rate and obtain an interval from 0.18 to 0.24.\n\nA frequentist interpretation is:\n\n> The procedure used to construct 95% confidence intervals would capture the true conversion rate in about 95% of repeated samples under the model assumptions.\n\nA Bayesian interpretation is:\n\n> Given the data, prior, and model, there is 95% posterior probability that the conversion rate lies within the specified credible interval.\n\n**Key difference:**\n\n```text\nConfidence interval:\nRepeated-sampling interpretation\n\nCredible interval:\nPosterior-probability interpretation\n```\n\nThe numerical intervals can sometimes be similar, but their interpretations and underlying frameworks are different.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> A confidence interval is a frequentist repeated-sampling construct, while a credible interval is Bayesian and represents posterior probability for a parameter given the data, model, and prior. The distinction is mainly about interpretation and statistical framework, not simply the percentage used.",
