    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables in a regression are highly linearly correlated with each other. It doesn't bias the model's overall predictions, but i...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can reason about statistical evidence correctly and avoid common interpretations that lead to poor analytical decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the statistical concept defined accurately.\n- They want you to explain the assumptions, limitations, or interpretation that matter in practice.\n- They want you to connect the result to a real analytical or business decision rather than treating a statistic as a standalone answer.",
    sampleAnswer: "Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables in a regression are highly linearly correlated with each other. It doesn't bias the model's overall predictions, but it inflates the variance of individual coefficient estimates, making them unstable and hard to interpret ? small changes in the data can swing coefficient signs and magnitudes, and standard errors become large, which can make genuinely important predictors look statistically insignificant. It's commonly detected with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF), where a VIF above roughly 5-10 is a common (though somewhat arbitrary) rule-of-thumb warning sign, or by inspecting the correlation matrix directly. Common remedies include dropping or combining redundant features, using dimensionality reduction like PCA, or switching to a regularized model like Ridge regression, which handles correlated predictors more gracefully than ordinary least squares.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nMulticollinearity occurs when predictor variables in a regression model are strongly linearly related to one another. It makes it difficult to distinguish the individual contribution of correlated predictors and can make coefficient estimates unstable and standard errors large.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a model predicts salary using:\n\n```text\nexperience_in_years\nage\nyears_since_graduation\n```\n\nThese variables may be strongly related. The model may have difficulty determining how much of the predicted salary should be attributed to each individual predictor.\n\n**Why it is a problem:**\n\n- Regression coefficients can become unstable.\n- Standard errors can increase.\n- Individual predictors may appear statistically insignificant even when the group is useful.\n- Coefficient signs or magnitudes can change substantially when variables are added or removed.\n- Interpretation of individual coefficients becomes difficult.\n\nMulticollinearity does not necessarily hurt prediction accuracy dramatically, especially when the correlated variables collectively contain useful information, but it can seriously affect coefficient interpretation and inference.\n\n**How to detect it:**\n\n- Inspect a correlation matrix for strongly correlated predictors.\n- Calculate the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF).\n- Look for unstable coefficients across model specifications.\n- Examine condition numbers or eigenvalue-based diagnostics for more complex cases.\n\nA common VIF definition is:\n\n```text\nVIF = 1 / (1 - R²)\n```\n\nwhere `R²` comes from regressing one predictor against the other predictors.\n\n**How to address it:**\n\n- Remove redundant predictors when justified by the problem domain.\n- Combine highly related variables into a meaningful feature.\n- Use dimensionality-reduction methods such as PCA when appropriate.\n- Use regularization such as ridge regression when prediction is the main goal.\n- Collect more informative data or redesign the features.\n- Keep correlated variables when they are substantively important, while acknowledging the uncertainty in individual coefficients.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Multicollinearity occurs when predictors are strongly related to each other. It can make regression coefficients unstable, increase standard errors, and make individual effects difficult to interpret. I would detect it using correlation analysis, VIF, and coefficient stability, then address it through feature selection, combining variables, dimensionality reduction, or regularization depending on whether interpretation or prediction is the primary goal.",
