    shortDescription: "Mean is the sum of values divided by the count, computable in a single pass. Median requires sorting the values (O(n log n) with Python's Timsort) and then taking the middle elemen...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can turn a data-manipulation requirement into clear, efficient Python and Pandas code while handling realistic edge cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want correct code with sensible handling of missing, empty, or duplicated data.\n- They want you to understand the behavior and complexity of the operations you choose.\n- They want an implementation that is readable and practical rather than unnecessarily clever.",
    sampleAnswer: "Mean is the sum of values divided by the count, computable in a single pass. Median requires sorting the values (O(n log n) with Python's Timsort) and then taking the middle element, or averaging the two middle elements for an even-length list. Mode requires counting frequencies, typically with a dictionary, in O(n) time, and then picking the key with the highest count ? being aware that a dataset can have more than one mode (a tie), which a naive max() over the dictionary will silently resolve to just one of them.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would implement each statistic using basic Python operations. The mean is the sum divided by the number of observations, the median requires sorting and selecting the middle value or averaging the two middle values, and the mode is the value with the highest frequency.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef mean(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"values cannot be empty\")\n    return sum(values) / len(values)\n\n\ndef median(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"values cannot be empty\")\n\n    values = sorted(values)\n    n = len(values)\n    middle = n // 2\n\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return values[middle]\n\n    return (values[middle - 1] + values[middle]) / 2\n\n\ndef mode(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"values cannot be empty\")\n\n    counts = {}\n    for value in values:\n        counts[value] = counts.get(value, 0) + 1\n\n    max_count = max(counts.values())\n    return [value for value, count in counts.items() if count == max_count]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\nvalues = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4]\n\nprint(mean(values))    # 2.4\nprint(median(values))  # 2\nprint(mode(values))    # [2]\n```\n\nThe median requires sorting, so its typical time complexity is `O(n log n)`. Mean is `O(n)`, and mode can be computed in `O(n)` average time using a dictionary, with `O(n)` additional space.\n\nThe mode implementation returns a list because multiple values can tie for the highest frequency.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Mean is computed as the sum divided by the number of observations. Median requires sorting and selecting the middle value, while mode requires counting frequencies and selecting the most frequent value or values. Mean and mode can be computed in linear time, while the straightforward median implementation requires `O(n log n)` time because of sorting.",
    keyPoints: ["They want correct code with sensible handling of missing, empty, or duplicated data.", "They want you to understand the behavior and complexity of the operations you choose.", "They want an implementation that is readable and practical rather than unnecessarily clever."],
