    shortDescription: "This is a single linear scan: track the best value seen so far and its index, updating whenever a strictly larger value is found. The important edge cases to mention out loud in an...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can turn a data-manipulation requirement into clear, efficient Python and Pandas code while handling realistic edge cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want correct code with sensible handling of missing, empty, or duplicated data.\n- They want you to understand the behavior and complexity of the operations you choose.\n- They want an implementation that is readable and practical rather than unnecessarily clever.",
    sampleAnswer: "This is a single linear scan: track the best value seen so far and its index, updating whenever a strictly larger value is found. The important edge cases to mention out loud in an interview are an empty list (should raise or return a sentinel, depending on the contract) and ties (the code below returns the first occurrence of the max, which should be stated explicitly since \"the index of the maximum\" is ambiguous when the max value repeats).",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would scan the list once while keeping track of the largest value and its index. This gives `O(n)` time complexity and `O(1)` additional space.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef index_of_max(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"list cannot be empty\")\n\n    max_index = 0\n\n    for i in range(1, len(values)):\n        if values[i] > values[max_index]:\n            max_index = i\n\n    return max_index\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\nvalues = [10, 4, 25, 7, 18]\nprint(index_of_max(values))  # 2\n```\n\nThe function returns the index of the first maximum when the maximum value appears more than once:\n\n```python\nvalues = [10, 25, 7, 25]\nprint(index_of_max(values))  # 1\n```\n\nThe function runs in `O(n)` time because every element may need to be examined once, and it uses `O(1)` additional space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would perform a single linear scan while storing the index of the largest value seen so far. The algorithm takes `O(n)` time and `O(1)` additional space and naturally returns the first index when multiple elements share the maximum value.",
