    subcategorySlug: "python-and-pandas",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Use str.split() with expand=True to turn the single column into separate columns in one step, rather than looping row by row, which is both slower and more error-prone. After the s...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can turn a data-manipulation requirement into clear, efficient Python and Pandas code while handling realistic edge cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want correct code with sensible handling of missing, empty, or duplicated data.\n- They want you to understand the behavior and complexity of the operations you choose.\n- They want an implementation that is readable and practical rather than unnecessarily clever.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use str.split() with expand=True to turn the single column into separate columns in one step, rather than looping row by row, which is both slower and more error-prone. After the split, decide explicitly how to handle rows where the name doesn't have exactly two parts (a single-word name, or a name with a middle name) ? a naive split can silently produce None in the second column or an extra column for a three-part name. Duplicate columns (from a bad merge or accidental re-run of a transformation) should be checked with df.columns.duplicated() and dropped deliberately, not assumed away. Missing values should be handled according to the actual data contract ? dropping, filling with a sentinel, or imputing ? rather than defaulting to one option without justification.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would first normalize missing values and whitespace, then split `full_name` using a clearly defined rule. If the requirement is simply first name plus everything after the first space as last name, I would split only once. I would explicitly decide how to handle single-word names and missing values, then check for duplicate column names before continuing with downstream processing.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"full_name\": [\n        \"Alice Smith\",\n        \"Bob Johnson Jr\",\n        None,\n        \"Charlie\"\n    ]\n})\n\nname_parts = df[\"full_name\"].fillna(\"\").str.strip().str.split(\" \", n=1, expand=True)\n\ndf[\"first_name\"] = name_parts[0].replace(\"\", pd.NA)\ndf[\"last_name\"] = name_parts[1].replace(\"\", pd.NA)\n```\n\nWith `n=1`, `Alice Smith` becomes `Alice` and `Smith`, while `Bob Johnson Jr` becomes `Bob` and `Johnson Jr`. A single-word name such as `Charlie` gets a missing `last_name` rather than incorrectly treating part of the name as another field.\n\n**Handling missing values:**\n\nI would decide whether missing `full_name` should remain missing, be rejected, or be converted to a placeholder based on the data contract. For analytics, preserving missingness as `NA` is often preferable to silently inventing values.\n\n**Handling duplicate columns:**\n\nBefore or after transformation, I would check column-name uniqueness:\n\n```python\nprint(df.columns[df.columns.duplicated()].tolist())\n```\n\nIf duplicate columns are accidental, I would remove or rename them according to an explicit rule rather than silently dropping information. For example, if only exact duplicate column names are unwanted:\n\n```python\ndf = df.loc[:, ~df.columns.duplicated()]\n```\n\nHowever, this keeps the first occurrence and discards later columns, so I would only do this when that behavior is known to be safe.\n\n**Important data-quality consideration:**\n\nSplitting names is inherently ambiguous. Names can contain multiple family names, prefixes, suffixes, or cultural naming conventions. For production data, I would define the expected format with the data owner rather than assuming that the first token is always the first name and the second token is always the last name.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would normalize whitespace and missing values, split `full_name` according to an explicit business rule, and preserve missing values rather than inventing data. I would then check for duplicate column names and resolve them deliberately. For production systems, I would avoid assuming that every person's name follows a simple first-name/last-name format because naming conventions vary.",
