    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "First quantify how much is missing and whether it's missing completely at random, at random conditional on other features, or not at random (e.g., high earners systematically decli...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can turn a data-manipulation requirement into clear, efficient Python and Pandas code while handling realistic edge cases.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want correct code with sensible handling of missing, empty, or duplicated data.\n- They want you to understand the behavior and complexity of the operations you choose.\n- They want an implementation that is readable and practical rather than unnecessarily clever.",
    sampleAnswer: "First quantify how much is missing and whether it's missing completely at random, at random conditional on other features, or not at random (e.g., high earners systematically declining to report income) ? the mechanism matters more than the percentage missing when deciding how to handle it. Dropping rows (dropna()) is reasonable when missingness is small and random; dropping a column is reasonable when a feature is missing too often to be useful. Imputing (fillna() with mean/median/mode, or a model-based imputer like KNNImputer or IterativeImputer) preserves sample size but can distort variance and relationships if applied carelessly, especially for data missing not at random. For features feeding a model, adding a binary \"was_missing\" indicator alongside imputed values sometimes preserves useful information that pure imputation would otherwise erase.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would first understand why the data are missing, how much is missing, and whether missingness is related to other variables or the target. Then I would choose between dropping, imputing, or explicitly modeling missingness based on the business context and downstream analysis. I would avoid automatically filling every missing value with zero or the mean because that can introduce bias or distort the data distribution.\n\n**Inspect missing values:**\n\n```python\nmissing = df.isna().sum()\nmissing_percentage = df.isna().mean() * 100\n```\n\n**Dropping rows:**\n\n```python\ndf_clean = df.dropna(subset=[\"age\", \"income\"])\n```\n\nDropping can be reasonable when only a small fraction of observations are missing and the remaining sample is still representative.\n\n**Dropping columns:**\n\n```python\ndf_clean = df.drop(columns=[\"mostly_missing_column\"])\n```\n\nThis may be appropriate when a feature has an extremely high missing rate and provides little reliable information.\n\n**Imputation:**\n\nFor numeric data, simple imputation might use the median:\n\n```python\ndf[\"age\"] = df[\"age\"].fillna(df[\"age\"].median())\n```\n\nFor categorical data, the most frequent category or an explicit `Unknown` category may be appropriate:\n\n```python\ndf[\"city\"] = df[\"city\"].fillna(\"Unknown\")\n```\n\nFor machine learning, more sophisticated methods such as KNN or model-based imputation may be appropriate depending on the problem.\n\n**Important considerations:**\n\n- Determine whether missingness is random or systematic.\n- Consider the percentage and pattern of missing values.\n- Understand whether missingness itself contains information.\n- Fit imputation values using the training data only to avoid data leakage.\n- Preserve missingness when it has meaningful business semantics.\n- Validate that the chosen strategy does not distort important distributions.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would first investigate the amount and mechanism of missingness rather than automatically dropping or filling values. I would drop rows or columns when the missing portion is small or the feature has little useful information, and use appropriate imputation when retaining observations is important. For machine learning, imputation must be fitted on the training set to prevent data leakage.",
