    slug: "15-write-a-sql-query-that-joins-two-tables-and-uses-a-subquery-to-filter-on-an-aggregated-condition-fro",
    categoryName: "SQL for Data Science",
    categorySlug: "data-science/sql-for-data-science",
    subcategoryName: "SQL for Data Science",
    subcategorySlug: "sql-for-data-science",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A common real-world pattern: join orders to customers to get a customer's details, but only include customers whose total order value exceeds some threshold ? that threshold check...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to evaluate whether you can write reliable analytical SQL and reason about grouping, joins, duplicates, ranking, and row-level versus aggregate results.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the query to produce the requested result without accidental row multiplication or incorrect aggregation.\n- They want you to explain why each SQL clause or window function is used.\n- They want you to recognize edge cases such as ties, duplicate rows, and the grain of each table.",
    sampleAnswer: "A common real-world pattern: join orders to customers to get a customer's details, but only include customers whose total order value exceeds some threshold ? that threshold check requires an aggregate, so it's expressed either as a subquery in the WHERE/HAVING clause or as a CTE joined back in. Before writing the query, it's worth explicitly stating the grain of each table (one row per order vs. one row per customer) so the join doesn't accidentally fan out rows and inflate later aggregates.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nSuppose we have `customers`, `orders`, and `payments`, and we want customers and their orders where the customer's total payments exceed 10,000. I would aggregate the third table in a subquery and then use that result to filter the joined tables.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    c.customer_id,\n    c.name,\n    o.order_id,\n    o.amount\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o\n    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE c.customer_id IN (\n    SELECT p.customer_id\n    FROM payments p\n    GROUP BY p.customer_id\n    HAVING SUM(p.amount) > 10000\n);\n```\n\nThe inner query groups `payments` by customer and keeps only customers whose total payment amount exceeds 10,000. The outer query then joins `customers` and `orders` and returns orders belonging to those customers.\n\n**Alternative using a derived table:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    c.customer_id,\n    c.name,\n    o.order_id,\n    o.amount\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o\n    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nJOIN (\n    SELECT customer_id\n    FROM payments\n    GROUP BY customer_id\n    HAVING SUM(amount) > 10000\n) p\n    ON p.customer_id = c.customer_id;\n```\n\nThe best form depends on readability, database optimizer behavior, and the complexity of the query.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would aggregate the third table in a subquery using `GROUP BY` and `HAVING`, then use the resulting keys to filter the joined tables. This cleanly separates the aggregation condition from the main query and can also be expressed as a join against a derived table.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the query to produce the requested result without accidental row multiplication or incorrect aggregation.", "They want you to explain why each SQL clause or window function is used.", "They want you to recognize edge cases such as ties, duplicate rows, and the grain of each table."],
