    shortDescription: "Precision is the fraction of items predicted positive that are actually positive (TP / (TP + FP)) ? it answers \"when the model says yes, how often is it right?\" Recall is the fract...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "Precision is the fraction of items predicted positive that are actually positive (TP / (TP + FP)) ? it answers \"when the model says yes, how often is it right?\" Recall is the fraction of actual positives the model successfully identifies (TP / (TP + FN)) ? it answers \"of all the real positives, how many did the model catch?\" There's usually a trade-off between them controlled by the classification threshold. You'd prioritize recall when missing a positive is costly (fraud detection, cancer screening, where a false negative can be severe), and prioritize precision when a false positive is costly or erodes trust (flagging a legitimate transaction as fraud and blocking a customer's card, or a spam filter that misclassifies important email).",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPrecision measures how many of the items predicted as positive are actually positive, while recall measures how many of all actual positive items the model successfully identifies. Precision is important when false positives are expensive, while recall is important when false negatives are expensive.\n\n**Formulas:**\n\n```text\nPrecision = True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives)\n\nRecall = True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a fraud detection system flags transactions as potentially fraudulent.\n\nIf the business prioritizes **precision**, it wants most flagged transactions to actually be fraud. This is useful when investigating a transaction is expensive or incorrectly blocking legitimate customers creates significant friction.\n\nIf the business prioritizes **recall**, it wants to catch as much fraud as possible, even if some legitimate transactions are flagged. This may be appropriate when missing a fraudulent transaction is much more costly than reviewing false alarms.\n\nAnother example is medical screening. If the goal is to avoid missing people who may have a serious disease, high recall is often prioritized, with follow-up testing used to deal with false positives.\n\n**Trade-off:**\n\nIncreasing the classification threshold often increases precision but decreases recall, while lowering it can increase recall at the expense of precision. The appropriate threshold should be chosen based on business costs, not simply because one metric is higher.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Precision answers, \"Of the cases I predicted positive, how many were actually positive?\" Recall answers, \"Of all the actual positive cases, how many did I find?\" I would prioritize precision when false positives are expensive and recall when false negatives are more costly. The decision should ultimately be based on the business cost of each type of error.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.", "They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.", "They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario."],
