    subcategorySlug: "core-machine-learning",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "ROC-AUC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across all classification thresholds and summarizes the curve as a single number between 0.5 (no better than random) an...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "ROC-AUC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across all classification thresholds and summarizes the curve as a single number between 0.5 (no better than random) and 1 (perfect separation); it measures how well the model ranks positives above negatives, independent of any single threshold. It's a poor choice, or at least an incomplete one, when classes are heavily imbalanced, because the false positive rate denominator (true negatives, which are abundant in an imbalanced dataset) can make the ROC curve look deceptively good even when precision on the minority class is poor. In that setting, a Precision-Recall AUC curve is usually more informative since it focuses specifically on how well the model performs on the positive (often minority) class.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nROC-AUC measures how well a classifier ranks positive examples above negative examples across different classification thresholds. The ROC curve plots true positive rate against false positive rate, and AUC summarizes the area under that curve. An AUC of 0.5 corresponds to random ranking, while 1.0 represents perfect ranking.\n\n**Why it is useful:**\n\nROC-AUC is threshold-independent and can be useful for comparing classifiers when the positive and negative classes have reasonably meaningful representation and the ranking quality across thresholds matters.\n\n**When it is not a good choice:**\n\nROC-AUC can be misleading with highly imbalanced datasets. For example, in fraud detection where only 0.1% of transactions are fraudulent, the false-positive rate can remain very small even when the model produces a large number of false positives in absolute terms.\n\nIn such cases, **PR-AUC (Precision-Recall AUC)** can provide a more informative view of performance on the positive class.\n\nROC-AUC is also insufficient when the business cares about one specific operating threshold or has very asymmetric costs. In that situation, I would examine precision, recall, confusion matrices, expected cost, calibration, and metrics at the actual operating point.\n\n**Important distinction:**\n\nROC-AUC evaluates ranking performance across thresholds; it does not tell you whether predicted probabilities are well calibrated.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> ROC-AUC measures how well a classifier ranks positives above negatives across thresholds. It can be less informative for highly imbalanced problems or when only a narrow operating region matters. In those cases, I would consider PR-AUC and threshold-specific metrics such as precision, recall, false-positive rate, expected cost, and calibration.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.", "They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.", "They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario."],
