    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "There's no single universally-best algorithm ? a good answer explains the reasoning for choosing one over others rather than just naming a favorite. For structured/tabular data, gr...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand how machine-learning methods behave and can choose or evaluate them based on the actual problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.\n- They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.\n- They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario.",
    sampleAnswer: "There's no single universally-best algorithm ? a good answer explains the reasoning for choosing one over others rather than just naming a favorite. For structured/tabular data, gradient-boosted decision trees (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) are a strong default because they handle nonlinear relationships and feature interactions well with limited preprocessing, at the cost of more hyperparameter tuning and less interpretability than a linear model. Logistic/linear regression remains a strong choice when interpretability matters (regulated industries, stakeholder trust) or the dataset is small. Neural networks generally need larger datasets to outperform tree-based methods on tabular data, but are the standard choice for unstructured data like images, text, and audio. The actual decision depends on data size, feature types, interpretability requirements, latency constraints, and the cost of different error types.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI do not choose a machine-learning algorithm based on a personal favorite. I start with the business objective, data characteristics, constraints, and evaluation metric, then establish a strong baseline and compare appropriate candidate models using a validation strategy that matches the data.\n\nFor many structured tabular problems, gradient-boosted decision trees are an excellent starting point because they can model nonlinear relationships and interactions with relatively little feature engineering. However, they are not automatically the best choice for every problem.\n\n**How I would decide:**\n\n1. Define the business objective and cost of errors.\n2. Determine whether the task is classification, regression, ranking, forecasting, clustering, or another problem.\n3. Understand dataset size, feature types, missingness, class imbalance, and noise.\n4. Establish a simple baseline such as linear/logistic regression or a simple tree model.\n5. Evaluate appropriate candidate algorithms.\n6. Use cross-validation or a time/group-aware validation strategy when appropriate.\n7. Compare models using business-relevant metrics.\n8. Consider latency, memory, interpretability, training cost, and operational complexity.\n9. Check calibration and robustness if predicted probabilities drive decisions.\n10. Prefer the simplest model that meets the business requirements.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n- Tabular classification: logistic regression, random forests, gradient boosting.\n- Text: linear models with strong representations, transformers, or other NLP models depending on scale and requirements.\n- Images: convolutional or transformer-based vision models.\n- Time series: statistical forecasting models, tree-based models with engineered temporal features, or specialized deep-learning approaches depending on the data.\n- Very small datasets: simpler models may outperform complex models because they have lower variance.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would not select an algorithm by preference alone. I would define the business objective and constraints, establish a simple baseline, evaluate suitable algorithms using an appropriate validation strategy, and choose based on generalization performance, business metrics, interpretability, latency, cost, and operational complexity. The best model is usually the simplest one that reliably meets the requirements.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the model concept or metric explained precisely.", "They want you to discuss trade-offs, assumptions, and situations where a method can mislead.", "They want you to connect model behavior to a concrete business or modeling scenario."],
