    subcategorySlug: "feature-engineering-and-data-preparation",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "One-hot encoding a high-cardinality column creates thousands of mostly-sparse columns, which bloats memory and can hurt tree-based models by diluting the signal across many rare ca...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because data preparation decisions can materially change model validity, generalization, and production behavior.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to identify the source of leakage, bias, or distorted feature behavior.\n- They want a preprocessing strategy that respects the training/validation boundary.\n- They want you to explain when different preprocessing choices are appropriate.",
    sampleAnswer: "One-hot encoding a high-cardinality column creates thousands of mostly-sparse columns, which bloats memory and can hurt tree-based models by diluting the signal across many rare categories. Better options depend on the model type: for tree-based models, target encoding (replacing each category with a statistic like the mean target value for that category, computed carefully with cross-validation or smoothing to avoid leakage) or frequency encoding often works well; for linear or neural models, learned embeddings (similar to word embeddings) can capture relationships between categories in a lower-dimensional space. Grouping rare categories into an \"other\" bucket is also a simple, effective baseline when many categories individually have very few observations.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would not automatically one-hot encode a categorical feature with 10,000 unique values because it can create a very large sparse feature matrix and may generalize poorly for rare categories. I would first understand what the category represents, how frequently categories occur, and whether there is meaningful structure that can be represented more efficiently.\n\n**Possible approaches:**\n\n- **Frequency/count encoding:** Replace each category with its occurrence count or frequency.\n- **Target encoding:** Replace categories with statistics related to the target, using careful out-of-fold or training-only computation to avoid leakage.\n- **Hashing:** Map categories into a fixed number of dimensions when the category space is very large.\n- **Learned embeddings:** Useful for neural networks or large-scale recommendation/entity problems.\n- **One-hot encoding:** Still reasonable when the cardinality is manageable and the downstream model handles sparse features efficiently.\n- **Grouping rare categories:** Combine infrequent categories into an `Other` group when appropriate.\n- **Domain-based aggregation:** Replace a high-cardinality identifier with meaningful attributes if possible.\n- **Tree-based native categorical handling:** Some model implementations can handle categorical features directly or provide specialized encodings.\n\n**Important considerations:**\n\nTarget encoding is particularly powerful but dangerous if calculated using the full dataset. For validation, each encoded training value must be calculated without using the validation target values. In cross-validation, target statistics should be generated out-of-fold for training examples and from the training partition when encoding validation data.\n\nAlso, if the feature is essentially a unique identifier, such as `user_id`, blindly encoding it may allow the model to memorize entities rather than learn generalizable patterns.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> For a 10,000-category feature, I would first determine whether it is a meaningful categorical variable or merely an identifier. Depending on the model and business problem, I might use frequency encoding, carefully implemented target encoding, hashing, embeddings, rare-category grouping, or native categorical handling. I would avoid leakage and evaluate whether the representation generalizes to unseen categories.",
