    subcategorySlug: "deep-learning-basics",
    experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
    difficulty: "HARD",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "For structured/tabular data with a moderate number of features and rows (thousands to a few million), gradient-boosted trees are usually the practical default ? they tend to match...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand the mechanics and practical limitations of neural-network training rather than only knowing model names.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the learning mechanism explained from inputs through loss and parameter updates.\n- They want you to identify the training problem or architectural trade-off involved.\n- They want a practical example showing how the concept affects model training or model selection.",
    sampleAnswer: "For structured/tabular data with a moderate number of features and rows (thousands to a few million), gradient-boosted trees are usually the practical default ? they tend to match or beat deep learning on tabular data, train faster, need less data and less hyperparameter tuning, and are easier to interpret with tools like SHAP. Deep learning tends to pull ahead when the data is unstructured (images, audio, raw text, video) where it can learn hierarchical feature representations that would otherwise require extensive manual feature engineering, or when the dataset is very large and complex relationships benefit from the extra model capacity. The decision should also weigh available compute, latency requirements at inference time, and how much labeled data actually exists, since deep learning generally needs substantially more data to reach its advantage over simpler methods.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would decide based on the data type, dataset size, amount of labeled data, feature structure, latency and infrastructure constraints, interpretability requirements, and expected business value. For structured/tabular data, gradient-boosted trees are often an excellent first choice. Deep learning becomes more attractive when the problem involves unstructured data such as images, audio, natural language, or very large datasets where representation learning can provide substantial value.\n\n**My decision process:**\n\n1. Define the business objective and success metric.\n2. Understand the data type and volume.\n3. Establish a simple baseline.\n4. Try a strong classical model for structured data.\n5. Consider deep learning if the data or problem benefits from learned representations.\n6. Compare models using an appropriate validation strategy.\n7. Consider latency, infrastructure cost, interpretability, training complexity, and maintenance.\n8. Choose the simplest approach that meets the business requirements.\n\n**Typical choices:**\n\n```text\nStructured/tabular data\n    -> Linear models / tree ensembles / gradient boosting\n\nImages\n    -> Deep-learning vision models\n\nText / speech\n    -> Transformer or other deep-learning models\n\nSmall dataset + structured features\n    -> Classical ML often preferable\n\nVery large unstructured dataset\n    -> Deep learning often more attractive\n```\n\n**Important point:**\n\nI would not assume that deep learning is automatically better. On many tabular business datasets, gradient boosting can provide excellent accuracy with less training data, lower infrastructure cost, faster experimentation, and easier interpretability.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would start with the problem and data rather than the algorithm. For structured tabular data, I would establish a baseline and usually evaluate gradient boosting early. For images, text, audio, or very large datasets where representation learning matters, I would consider deep learning. The final choice should balance predictive performance with data requirements, latency, interpretability, cost, and operational complexity.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the learning mechanism explained from inputs through loss and parameter updates.", "They want you to identify the training problem or architectural trade-off involved.", "They want a practical example showing how the concept affects model training or model selection."],
