    question: "What is Simpson's Paradox, and how could it mislead an A/B test analysis?",
    slug: "35-what-is-simpson-s-paradox-and-how-could-it-mislead-an-a-b-test-analysis",
    categoryName: "Experimentation and A/B Testing",
    categorySlug: "data-science/experimentation-and-ab-testing",
    subcategoryName: "Experimentation and A/B Testing",
    subcategorySlug: "experimentation-and-ab-testing",
    experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
    difficulty: "HARD",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Simpson's Paradox occurs when a trend that appears in several different groups of data disappears, or reverses, when the groups are combined ? usually because of an unevenly distri...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to determine whether you can design experiments that produce trustworthy evidence instead of conclusions driven by statistical artifacts.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the experimental design and statistical issue explained clearly.\n- They want you to identify sources of bias, false positives, or misleading aggregation.\n- They want a concrete example of how the issue could change a product decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "Simpson's Paradox occurs when a trend that appears in several different groups of data disappears, or reverses, when the groups are combined ? usually because of an unevenly distributed confounding variable across the groups. In A/B testing, this can happen when the randomization ratio or traffic mix shifted partway through the test (e.g., more mobile users entered later, and mobile users convert differently), making the aggregate result misleading even though each individual segment's result is directionally consistent, or vice versa ? an aggregate showing a lift while every individual segment actually shows a decline.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nSimpson's Paradox occurs when the relationship observed in aggregated data reverses or substantially changes after the data are divided into meaningful subgroups. In an A/B test, an overall treatment effect can therefore be misleading if treatment and control groups differ in the composition of important subgroups or if subgroup-level behavior is very different.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a new feature is tested across two user groups:\n\n```text\nNew users      -> Treatment performs better\nExperienced    -> Treatment performs better\n```\n\nYet after combining the groups, the overall result could appear worse if the treatment group contains a very different proportion of new and experienced users.\n\nThis can happen because aggregation changes the weighting of the groups.\n\n**How I would investigate it:**\n\n- Verify randomization and treatment allocation.\n- Compare treatment/control composition across important pre-treatment segments.\n- Analyze predefined important subgroups.\n- Look for confounding variables and changes in traffic mix.\n- Avoid indiscriminately slicing the data into many subgroups because that introduces multiple-testing problems.\n- Use an appropriate statistical model when adjustment for important covariates is justified.\n\nA properly randomized experiment should generally protect against systematic baseline imbalance in expectation, but random imbalance can still occur in finite samples, and implementation or eligibility problems can create more serious issues.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Simpson's Paradox occurs when an aggregate relationship differs from, or even reverses, the relationships observed within meaningful subgroups. In A/B testing, I would check randomization, treatment/control composition, and important predefined segments before concluding that the aggregate treatment effect represents all users.",
    keyPoints: ["They want the experimental design and statistical issue explained clearly.", "They want you to identify sources of bias, false positives, or misleading aggregation.", "They want a concrete example of how the issue could change a product decision."],
