    question: "A stakeholder asks you to build a churn-prediction model. Walk through how you would approach the problem end to end.",
    slug: "36-a-stakeholder-asks-you-to-build-a-churn-prediction-model-walk-through-how-you-would-approach-the-pro",
    categoryName: "Case Studies and Applied Scenarios",
    categorySlug: "data-science/case-studies-and-applied-scenarios",
    subcategoryName: "Case Studies and Applied Scenarios",
    subcategorySlug: "case-studies-and-applied-scenarios",
    experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
    difficulty: "HARD",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Start by clarifying the business objective with the stakeholder: what action will actually be taken based on the model's predictions (a retention offer, an outreach call), since th...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can connect technical data-science work to a measurable business objective and make defensible modeling decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to start from the business objective, data constraints, and success metric.\n- They want you to justify technical choices and acknowledge reasonable alternatives.\n- They want a practical path from analysis or modeling to validation and measurable impact.",
    sampleAnswer: "Start by clarifying the business objective with the stakeholder: what action will actually be taken based on the model's predictions (a retention offer, an outreach call), since that determines what \"churn\" means (a specific definition and time window) and what error trade-off matters (false negatives, missing a customer who will churn, versus false positives, wasting a retention offer on someone who wouldn't have churned anyway). Define the prediction grain and the exact label window (e.g., \"will this customer churn in the next 30 days\"), assemble features using only information available as of the prediction date to avoid leakage, and choose an evaluation metric aligned to the business cost (often precision/recall or expected value at a chosen threshold rather than plain accuracy, since churn is usually imbalanced). Build a simple baseline first (logistic regression or a basic tree-based model) before reaching for something more complex, validate with a properly time-aware split if the data has a temporal component, and finally connect the model's output back to an actionable threshold and a plan for measuring the actual business impact of acting on its predictions, ideally validated with a holdout or randomized rollout rather than assumed from offline metrics alone.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would treat churn prediction as a business decision problem first and a modeling problem second. I would define exactly what churn means, when the prediction must be made, what action the business will take, and what cost is associated with false positives and false negatives. Then I would construct leakage-safe features, train and validate models using a time-aware strategy when appropriate, evaluate both predictive and business metrics, deploy the model, and monitor it continuously.\n\n**End-to-end approach:**\n\n1. **Define churn:** For example, cancellation or no activity for a specified period.\n2. **Define prediction point:** Establish exactly when the prediction is generated.\n3. **Define prediction horizon:** For example, predict whether a customer will churn within the next 30 days.\n4. **Define business action:** Decide what happens to customers predicted to be at risk.\n5. **Define success metrics:** Consider recall, precision, PR-AUC, calibration, lift, expected profit, retention rate, or incremental retention from interventions.\n6. **Collect data:** Usage, transactions, tenure, support interactions, product engagement, plan information, and other legitimate pre-prediction signals.\n7. **Prevent leakage:** Ensure every feature would have been available at prediction time.\n8. **Split data:** Use temporal or entity-aware splits when appropriate.\n9. **Establish a baseline:** Start with a simple model such as logistic regression.\n10. **Train stronger models:** Evaluate models such as gradient-boosted trees.\n11. **Tune using validation data:** Avoid using the final test set for model selection.\n12. **Evaluate calibration:** A churn probability should be meaningful if it drives risk-based decisions.\n13. **Choose an operating threshold:** Based on intervention capacity and business costs.\n14. **Run an intervention experiment:** Prediction accuracy alone does not prove that retention actions improve business outcomes.\n15. **Deploy:** Build a reliable batch or real-time scoring pipeline.\n16. **Monitor:** Track data drift, prediction distributions, model performance, calibration, latency, and business outcomes.\n17. **Retrain:** Establish a process based on drift and performance rather than an arbitrary schedule alone.\n\n**Important distinction:**\n\nA churn model tells us who is likely to churn. It does not automatically tell us who will respond positively to a retention intervention. For that, uplift or causal modeling and controlled experiments may be needed.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would define churn, the prediction timestamp, prediction horizon, business action, and success metrics first. Then I would build leakage-safe features, use an appropriate temporal validation strategy, establish a baseline, compare stronger models, calibrate probabilities, and select an operating threshold based on business costs. After deployment, I would monitor model and business performance and validate whether interventions actually reduce churn through experimentation.",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to start from the business objective, data constraints, and success metric.", "They want you to justify technical choices and acknowledge reasonable alternatives.", "They want a practical path from analysis or modeling to validation and measurable impact."],
