    experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
    difficulty: "HARD",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Structure it around the actual decision points, not just the final architecture: what was the business problem and how was success measured, what data was available and what were i...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to assess whether you can connect technical data-science work to a measurable business objective and make defensible modeling decisions.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to start from the business objective, data constraints, and success metric.\n- They want you to justify technical choices and acknowledge reasonable alternatives.\n- They want a practical path from analysis or modeling to validation and measurable impact.",
    sampleAnswer: "Structure it around the actual decision points, not just the final architecture: what was the business problem and how was success measured, what data was available and what were its limitations, what approaches were considered and why one was chosen over the alternatives, what went wrong or had to be iterated on, and what the measured outcome was once deployed (or why it wasn't deployed, if that's the honest answer). Interviewers probe project descriptions specifically to separate genuine ownership from a rehearsed summary, so being ready to go one level deeper on any single decision ? why that evaluation metric, why that specific feature, what happened when the model was retrained ? matters more than the polish of the initial summary.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would explain the project using a structured story that connects the business problem to measurable outcomes and then be prepared to justify every major technical decision. I would avoid presenting a list of technologies and instead explain why each decision was made, what alternatives were considered, and what trade-offs resulted.\n\n**Recommended structure:**\n\n```text\n1. Problem and business impact\n2. Data and scale\n3. Target definition\n4. Feature engineering\n5. Baseline\n6. Model selection\n7. Validation strategy\n8. Evaluation metrics\n9. Error analysis\n10. Deployment\n11. Monitoring\n12. Results\n13. Lessons learned\n```\n\nFor every major decision, I would be ready to answer:\n\n```text\nWhy this approach?\nWhat alternatives did you consider?\nWhat assumptions did you make?\nHow did you validate the decision?\nWhat could go wrong?\nWhat would you change now?\n```\n\nFor example, if I say I used gradient boosting, I should be able to explain why it was suitable for the data, what baseline I compared it against, how I tuned it, how I prevented leakage, how I handled imbalance, what metrics I used, and how I knew the improvement was real.\n\nI would also clearly distinguish what I personally implemented from what the broader team implemented.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would tell the project as a business-to-production story: problem, data, target, features, baseline, model choice, validation, metrics, deployment, monitoring, and results. For every important technical decision, I would explain the alternatives, assumptions, evidence, and trade-offs. I would also be explicit about my personal contribution so detailed follow-up questions can be answered honestly and precisely.",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to start from the business objective, data constraints, and success metric.", "They want you to justify technical choices and acknowledge reasonable alternatives.", "They want a practical path from analysis or modeling to validation and measurable impact."],
    commonMistakes: [],
