    categoryName: "Data Science Coding Practice",
    categorySlug: "data-science/data-science-coding-practice",
    subcategoryName: "Data Science Coding Practice",
    subcategorySlug: "data-science-coding-practice",
    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A decision tree recursively partitions the training data into subsets that are progressively more homogeneous with respect to the target. For classification, a common split-quality...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the underlying data-science concept and can apply it correctly to a realistic problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise explanation of the core idea.\n- They want you to identify the important assumption, trade-off, or edge case.\n- They want a practical example showing how you would use the concept.",
    sampleAnswer: "A decision tree recursively partitions the training data into subsets that are progressively more homogeneous with respect to the target. For classification, a common split-quality criterion is Gini impurity or entropy; for regression, variance reduction. Building it from scratch requires: evaluating candidate features and thresholds, scoring each candidate split by the weighted impurity of the resulting child nodes, picking the best split, recursing on each child, and stopping based on a condition like maximum depth, minimum samples per leaf, or no further impurity reduction. The computationally expensive part is evaluating many feature/threshold combinations at each node, which real implementations optimize with sorting and incremental impurity updates rather than recomputing from scratch for every candidate threshold.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFor a classification decision tree, I would recursively split the dataset into smaller groups. At each node, I would evaluate candidate features and thresholds, choose the split that produces the greatest reduction in impurity, recursively build child nodes, and stop when a stopping condition such as maximum depth, minimum samples, or zero impurity is reached.\n\n**Core algorithm:**\n\n```text\nStart with all training data\n        |\nCalculate impurity\n        |\nTry candidate feature/threshold splits\n        |\nChoose split with largest impurity reduction\n        |\n      /   \\\\\n   Left   Right\n    |       |\n Recurse  Recurse\n    |       |\n Stop when stopping condition is reached\n```\n\n**Simplified implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef gini(y):\n    if not y:\n        return 0.0\n\n    counts = {}\n    for label in y:\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\n\n    n = len(y)\n    return 1 - sum((count / n) ** 2 for count in counts.values())\n\n\ndef split_data(X, y, feature, threshold):\n    left_X, left_y = [], []\n    right_X, right_y = [], []\n\n    for row, label in zip(X, y):\n        if row[feature] <= threshold:\n            left_X.append(row)\n            left_y.append(label)\n        else:\n            right_X.append(row)\n            right_y.append(label)\n\n    return left_X, left_y, right_X, right_y\n```\n\nI would then calculate the weighted child impurity:\n\n```python\nweighted_gini = (\n    len(left_y) / len(y) * gini(left_y)\n    + len(right_y) / len(y) * gini(right_y)\n)\n```\n\nThe best split minimizes the weighted child impurity, or equivalently maximizes impurity reduction.\n\nA recursive node would contain either a prediction for a leaf or the selected feature, threshold, and references to its child nodes.\n\n**Stopping conditions:**\n\n- Maximum tree depth reached.\n- Too few samples to split.\n- Node is already pure.\n- No candidate split improves impurity sufficiently.\n\nFor a leaf, I would predict the majority class.\n\n**Prediction:**\n\n```python\ndef predict_one(node, row):\n    if node.is_leaf:\n        return node.prediction\n\n    if row[node.feature] <= node.threshold:\n        return predict_one(node.left, row)\n\n    return predict_one(node.right, row)\n```\n\nA production implementation would need efficient data structures, handling for missing values and categorical features, pruning or regularization, tie-breaking, efficient split search, and safeguards against pathological inputs.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would implement a decision tree recursively. At each node, I would evaluate candidate feature/threshold splits, calculate the weighted impurity of the resulting children, select the best split, and recursively repeat the process. I would stop based on depth, sample count, purity, or minimum improvement, and use the majority class at leaves. Prediction then follows the learned split path from the root to a leaf.",
