    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the underlying data-science concept and can apply it correctly to a realistic problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise explanation of the core idea.\n- They want you to identify the important assumption, trade-off, or edge case.\n- They want a practical example showing how you would use the concept.",
    sampleAnswer: "Gini impurity for a node with class probabilities p1...pk is 1 minus the sum of each pi squared; it's 0 when a node is perfectly pure (all one class) and higher as classes become more mixed. A tree evaluates candidate splits and picks the one that most reduces the weighted impurity of the resulting children compared to the parent. Maximum depth limits how many times the tree can split, directly controlling model complexity ? deeper trees can capture more intricate patterns but overfit more easily. Minimum samples per split or per leaf prevents the tree from creating splits based on very few observations, and post-training pruning (cost-complexity pruning) can remove branches that don't meaningfully improve validation performance, all serving the same goal of controlling the bias-variance tradeoff for tree-based models.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nGini impurity measures how mixed the classes are within a decision-tree node. A node containing only one class has Gini impurity of zero, while a more evenly mixed node has higher impurity. The tree chooses splits that reduce impurity. Hyperparameters such as maximum depth, minimum samples per split, minimum samples per leaf, and minimum impurity decrease control how aggressively the tree grows.\n\n**Gini impurity:**\n\n```text\nGini = 1 - sum(p_i²)\n```\n\nwhere `p_i` is the proportion of observations belonging to class `i` in the node.\n\nFor a binary node containing 80% class A and 20% class B:\n\n```text\nGini = 1 - (0.8² + 0.2²)\n     = 0.32\n```\n\nA pure node such as 100% class A has:\n\n```text\nGini = 1 - 1² = 0\n```\n\n**Controlling tree complexity:**\n\n- `max_depth`: Limits how many levels the tree can grow.\n- `min_samples_split`: Requires a minimum number of samples before splitting a node.\n- `min_samples_leaf`: Prevents leaves from becoming too small.\n- `max_leaf_nodes`: Limits the number of leaves.\n- `min_impurity_decrease`: Requires a split to provide sufficient improvement.\n- Feature subsampling can also reduce correlation and complexity in ensemble settings.\n\n**Overfitting relationship:**\n\n```text\nVery shallow tree -> high bias / underfitting\nVery deep tree    -> high variance / overfitting\n```\n\nI would tune these hyperparameters using cross-validation or an appropriate validation strategy rather than choosing them based only on training accuracy.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Gini impurity measures class mixing within a tree node. The tree selects splits that reduce weighted impurity. Parameters such as `max_depth`, `min_samples_split`, `min_samples_leaf`, and `max_leaf_nodes` control complexity. A very shallow tree can underfit, while an unrestricted deep tree can overfit, so I would tune complexity using appropriate validation data.",
