    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the underlying data-science concept and can apply it correctly to a realistic problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise explanation of the core idea.\n- They want you to identify the important assumption, trade-off, or edge case.\n- They want a practical example showing how you would use the concept.",
    sampleAnswer: "The iterative approach walks the list once, keeping track of the previous, current, and next nodes, and reverses each pointer as it goes ? O(n) time, O(1) extra space. The recursive approach recurses to the end of the list first, then reverses the pointer between each pair of nodes as the recursion unwinds ? also O(n) time, but O(n) space for the call stack, which matters for interview discussion since a very long list could risk a stack overflow with the recursive version in a language without tail-call optimization.",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nTo reverse a singly linked list, I need to reverse each node's `next` pointer. The iterative approach uses three pointers and runs in `O(n)` time with `O(1)` additional space. The recursive approach also takes `O(n)` time but uses `O(n)` call-stack space.\n\n\n\n**Node definition:**\n\n```python\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, value, next=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = next\n```\n\n**Iterative approach:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_iterative(head):\n    previous = None\n    current = head\n\n    while current:\n        next_node = current.next\n        current.next = previous\n        previous = current\n        current = next_node\n\n    return previous\n```\n\nThe important step is saving `current.next` before changing the pointer; otherwise, the rest of the list could be lost.\n\n**Recursive approach:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_recursive(head):\n    if head is None or head.next is None:\n        return head\n\n    new_head = reverse_recursive(head.next)\n\n    head.next.next = head\n    head.next = None\n\n    return new_head\n```\n\nThe recursive version reverses the remainder of the list first, then attaches the current node to the end of the reversed portion.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\n```text\nIterative:\nTime  = O(n)\nSpace = O(1)\n\nRecursive:\nTime  = O(n)\nSpace = O(n) call stack\n```\n\nFor production code, I would generally prefer the iterative version for a potentially long list because it avoids recursion-depth limitations.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> The iterative solution walks through the list while maintaining `previous`, `current`, and `next_node` pointers and reverses each link in place. It takes `O(n)` time and `O(1)` extra space. The recursive solution reverses the remainder of the list and then reconnects the current node, taking `O(n)` time and `O(n)` call-stack space.",
