    sampleAnswer: "A stack-based approach handles this naturally: push every opening bracket onto a stack, and for every closing bracket, check that it matches the type on top of the stack (popping it if so); if the stack is empty when a closing bracket appears, or the top doesn't match, or the stack isn't empty at the end, the string is invalid. This runs in O(n) time and O(n) space in the worst case (all opening brackets).",
detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nI would use a stack. Whenever I encounter an opening bracket, I push it onto the stack. For a closing bracket, I check whether the stack contains the corresponding opening bracket. If not, the string is invalid. At the end, the stack must be empty.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef is_valid_parentheses(s):\n    stack = []\n    pairs = {\n        ')': '(',\n        ']': '[',\n        '}': '{'\n    }\n\n    for char in s:\n        if char in \"([{\":\n            stack.append(char)\n        elif char in pairs:\n            if not stack or stack[-1] != pairs[char]:\n                return False\n            stack.pop()\n        else:\n            return False\n\n    return not stack\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```python\nprint(is_valid_parentheses(\"()[]{}\"))  # True\nprint(is_valid_parentheses(\"([{}])\"))  # True\nprint(is_valid_parentheses(\"([)]\"))    # False\nprint(is_valid_parentheses(\"((\"))      # False\n```\n\nFor `([)]`, when `)` is encountered, the top of the stack is `[`, not `(`, so the function immediately returns `False`.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\n```text\nTime  = O(n)\nSpace = O(n)\n```\n\nEach character is processed once, and in the worst case the stack can contain almost the entire input.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> I would use a stack because brackets must close in reverse order of how they opened. Push opening brackets, and for every closing bracket verify that it matches the stack's top element before popping it. The string is valid only if no mismatch occurs and the stack is empty at the end. The solution is `O(n)` time and `O(n)` space.",
